Task 1: Set Up Jaeger Tracing for Services
Subtask 1.1: Verify Service Mesh Installation
First, let's verify that OpenShift Service Mesh is properly installed and configured.

Check Service Mesh Control Plane:
oc get smcp -n istio-system
Verify Jaeger installation:
oc get jaeger -n istio-system
Check if Jaeger pods are running:
oc get pods -n istio-system | grep jaeger
Expected output should show Jaeger collector, query, and other components in Running state.

Subtask 1.2: Deploy Sample Microservices Application
We'll deploy a sample bookinfo application to demonstrate distributed tracing.

Create a new project for our application:
oc new-project bookinfo-tracing
Label the namespace for Service Mesh injection:
oc label namespace bookinfo-tracing istio-injection=enabled
Create Service Mesh Member Roll:
cat << EOF | oc apply -f -
apiVersion: maistra.io/v1
kind: ServiceMeshMemberRoll
metadata:
  name: default
  namespace: istio-system
spec:
  members:
  - bookinfo-tracing
EOF
Deploy the bookinfo application:
oc apply -f https://raw.githubusercontent.com/istio/istio/release-1.17/samples/bookinfo/platform/kube/bookinfo.yaml -n bookinfo-tracing
Verify all pods are running:
oc get pods -n bookinfo-tracing
Wait until all pods show Running status and 2/2 ready containers (indicating sidecar injection).

Subtask 1.3: Configure Jaeger Tracing
Create a Gateway for external access:
cat << EOF | oc apply -f -
apiVersion: networking.istio.io/v1beta1
kind: Gateway
metadata:
  name: bookinfo-gateway
  namespace: bookinfo-tracing
spec:
  selector:
    istio: ingressgateway
  servers:
  - port:
      number: 80
      name: http
      protocol: HTTP
    hosts:
    - "*"
EOF
Create VirtualService for routing:
cat << EOF | oc apply -f -
apiVersion: networking.istio.io/v1beta1
kind: VirtualService
metadata:
  name: bookinfo
  namespace: bookinfo-tracing
spec:
  hosts:
  - "*"
  gateways:
  - bookinfo-gateway
  http:
  - match:
    - uri:
        exact: /productpage
    - uri:
        prefix: /static
    - uri:
        exact: /login
    - uri:
        exact: /logout
    - uri:
        prefix: /api/v1/products
    route:
    - destination:
        host: productpage
        port:
          number: 9080
EOF
Configure tracing sampling rate (optional - for more detailed traces):
cat << EOF | oc apply -f -
apiVersion: install.istio.io/v1alpha1
kind: IstioOperator
metadata:
  name: tracing-config
  namespace: istio-system
spec:
  values:
    pilot:
      traceSampling: 100.0
EOF
Subtask 1.4: Enable Application-Level Tracing
Add tracing headers to the productpage service:
oc get deployment productpage-v1 -n bookinfo-tracing -o yaml > productpage-deployment.yaml
Edit the deployment to add tracing environment variables:
cat << EOF | oc apply -f -
apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: Deployment
metadata:
  name: productpage-v1
  namespace: bookinfo-tracing
spec:
  replicas: 1
  selector:
    matchLabels:
      app: productpage
      version: v1
  template:
    metadata:
      labels:
        app: productpage
        version: v1
    spec:
      containers:
      - name: productpage
        image: docker.io/istio/examples-bookinfo-productpage-v1:1.17.0
        imagePullPolicy: IfNotPresent
        ports:
        - containerPort: 9080
        env:
        - name: JAEGER_AGENT_HOST
          value: "jaeger-agent.istio-system.svc.cluster.local"
        - name: JAEGER_AGENT_PORT
          value: "6831"
        - name: JAEGER_SAMPLER_TYPE
          value: "const"
        - name: JAEGER_SAMPLER_PARAM
          value: "1"
EOF
Task 2: Analyze Traces in the Jaeger UI
Subtask 2.1: Access the Jaeger UI
Get the Jaeger route:
oc get route jaeger -n istio-system
If no route exists, create one:
oc expose service jaeger-query -n istio-system
Get the Jaeger UI URL:
echo "Jaeger UI: http://$(oc get route jaeger-query -n istio-system -o jsonpath='{.spec.host}')"
Open the Jaeger UI in your browser using the URL from the previous command.
Subtask 2.2: Generate Traffic for Tracing
Get the application URL:
export GATEWAY_URL=$(oc get route istio-ingressgateway -n istio-system -o jsonpath='{.spec.host}')
echo "Application URL: http://$GATEWAY_URL/productpage"
Generate some traffic to create traces:
for i in {1..20}; do
  curl -s "http://$GATEWAY_URL/productpage" > /dev/null
  echo "Request $i sent"
  sleep 2
done
Generate traffic with different patterns:
# Generate some failed requests
for i in {1..5}; do
  curl -s "http://$GATEWAY_URL/productpage?error=true" > /dev/null
  echo "Error request $i sent"
  sleep 1
done

# Generate requests with delays
for i in {1..10}; do
  curl -s "http://$GATEWAY_URL/productpage?delay=2" > /dev/null
  echo "Delayed request $i sent"
  sleep 3
done
